 ## Caveats
 
-The distribution is deliberately easy for an implementation that uses the
-displayed group action; that is the Track-B compact route, not a hidden attack.
-The hardness claim is only a no-tool compression gap, never worst-case
-complexity.  The G4 prior is uniform over twelve-subsets of already valid,
-balanced displayed rows; it accounts for obvious shape constraints but does
-not model a symmetry-aware solver, which would concentrate on the planted
-orbit.  No external Magma/nauty implementation was tested; the local exact
-branch-and-bound is the domain-standard substitute.  The canonical key uses
-stable colour refinement plus group-orbit profiles rather than complete
-isometry testing, so rare nonisomorphic collisions are possible.
+This distribution is intentionally easy for code that constructs the full
+compatibility graph; that is the disclosed Track-B baseline. G4 samples uniform
+12-subsets of already balanced, displayed rows. It accounts for the obvious
+shape constraints but not for a solver that exploits the supplied group action
+or compatibility degrees. The extra switching mates beyond 126 candidates can
+be canonicalized away by a tool, so those higher rungs mainly increase no-tool
+crowding and should not be mistaken for asymptotic complexity evidence.
 
